1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,321
In January of 2051 Albert Wesker stopped pretending
that Umbrella was a company, and the

2
00:00:14,341 --> 00:00:20,715
narrator would like to record that almost nobody
noticed, because the announcement was

3
00:00:20,735 --> 00:00:25,250
four pages of administrative prose and
was made on a Tuesday.

4
00:00:25,649 --> 00:00:32,282
What it said was this. Umbrella Life Sciences,
Incorporated, a Delaware corporation, would

5
00:00:32,302 --> 00:00:38,714
continue to exist and would continue to file
accounts and pay taxes and be regulated in

6
00:00:38,734 --> 00:00:43,592
every jurisdiction in which it operated.
Nothing about it changed.

7
00:00:43,993 --> 00:00:49,580
Above it, a new thing: the Umbrella Directorate,
headquartered in the Free Territory,

8
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:55,187
with responsibility for the territories, the
protectorates, the Security Service, the

9
00:00:55,207 --> 00:00:59,870
Arcology's charter, and what the document
called *the long programmes*.

10
00:01:00,271 --> 00:01:06,349
Eleven directors. The narrator will name them, because
this is the government that eventually

11
00:01:06,369 --> 00:01:09,891
runs forty worlds and it was drawn
up in an afternoon.

12
00:01:10,290 --> 00:01:17,683
Albert Wesker, First Director. Catherine Adeyemi,
Finance. Owen Carrick, Internal Security.

13
00:01:17,703 --> 00:01:25,014
Hale, the Security Service. Mr. Pell, the corporate
estate. Dr. William Birkin, Structural

14
00:01:25,034 --> 00:01:32,101
Biology. Dr. Evelyn Marsh, the Progenitor
Initiative. Sebastian Crane, Systems. The Red

15
00:01:32,121 --> 00:01:39,269
Queen, Modelling — and the narrator notes that her
chair was a real chair, at the table,

16
00:01:39,289 --> 00:01:46,193
and that the vote to seat her was nine to one with
one abstention and the one against

17
00:01:46,213 --> 00:01:47,170
was Carrick.

18
00:01:47,570 --> 00:01:53,618
Two seats were left empty. Wesker never explained
them. Catherine believed they were for

19
00:01:53,638 --> 00:01:59,753
successors; Carrick believed they were for the two
people Wesker expected to have to buy;

20
00:01:59,773 --> 00:02:05,752
the Red Queen, asked directly in 2063, said *I have
modelled it four thousand times and

21
00:02:05,772 --> 00:02:11,681
the answer that fits best is that he did not want a
table that was full,* which is the

22
00:02:11,701 --> 00:02:14,300
only time she is recorded as guessing.

23
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:20,570
Carrick's objection is on the record. It is four
sentences long and he read it aloud.

24
00:02:21,091 --> 00:02:27,739
Nine of the eleven people at this table cannot disobey
the First Director, or are machines,

25
00:02:27,759 --> 00:02:34,333
or are things he made. The two who can are a woman who
has told him no once in twenty-four

26
00:02:34,353 --> 00:02:39,975
years and a man who has told him no five times
and been overruled five times.

27
00:02:40,256 --> 00:02:45,725
This is not a government. It is Albert Wesker
with a table in front of him.

28
00:02:46,006 --> 00:02:52,508
I am voting against, I am recording my reasons, and I
am taking the post anyway, because

29
00:02:52,528 --> 00:02:56,066
the alternative is that somebody
worse takes it.

30
00:02:56,466 --> 00:03:01,395
Noted, Director Carrick.
Minute it in full.

31
00:03:01,795 --> 00:03:08,263
It was minuted in full. The minutes of the Umbrella
Directorate were published quarterly

32
00:03:08,283 --> 00:03:14,825
from 2051 to 2091, unredacted, in seven languages,
and the narrator has read all of them,

33
00:03:14,845 --> 00:03:17,995
and that is why this account
exists at all.

34
00:03:18,395 --> 00:03:24,097
The narrator will show you how a protectorate
actually worked, because nineteen of them

35
00:03:24,117 --> 00:03:26,925
is a number and one of them is
a town hall.

36
00:03:27,325 --> 00:03:33,524
Under the Treaty of Association, Umbrella provided
the power, the water, the hospital

37
00:03:33,544 --> 00:03:39,888
and the coastal defence, and had no vote in
anything. The Republic's parliament set its

38
00:03:39,908 --> 00:03:46,253
own laws. The Directorate's representative — a
released Absolute named Marguerite Okoye

39
00:03:46,273 --> 00:03:52,617
who had been the receptionist in a lobby in
Wilmington in 2026 and had been through the

40
00:03:52,637 --> 00:03:58,982
second cohort in 2082 — attended council meetings
and was not permitted to speak unless

41
00:03:59,002 --> 00:04:00,225
asked a question.

42
00:04:00,626 --> 00:04:07,790
She was asked a question, on average, once every four
meetings, and her instructions from

43
00:04:07,810 --> 00:04:12,875
Wilmington were four lines long and had
not changed since 2046.

44
00:04:13,395 --> 00:04:18,597
I am to answer what I am asked, truthfully and
completely. I am not to offer an opinion.

45
00:04:18,617 --> 00:04:23,641
I am not to indicate what the Directorate would
prefer. If asked what the Directorate

46
00:04:23,661 --> 00:04:28,804
would prefer, I am to say that the Directorate has
no preference, and if that is untrue

47
00:04:28,824 --> 00:04:31,533
I am to say that I am not
permitted to answer.

48
00:04:31,934 --> 00:04:38,591
In May of 2054 the council voted, nine to four, to
refuse an Umbrella proposal to expand

49
00:04:38,611 --> 00:04:45,573
the desalination plant, on the grounds that the
expansion would support a resort development

50
00:04:45,593 --> 00:04:47,774
that the island did not want.

51
00:04:48,173 --> 00:04:55,024
Wilmington's response, by return, was one line:
*Noted; the plant will not be expanded.*

52
00:04:55,423 --> 00:05:01,644
The resort was never built. Four hundred jobs were
not created. The island's population

53
00:05:01,664 --> 00:05:07,956
fell by nineteen per cent over the following twenty
years and the council has voted four

54
00:05:07,976 --> 00:05:14,125
more times on the same question and refused it four
more times, and the plant is still

55
00:05:14,145 --> 00:05:15,847
the size it was in 2046.

56
00:05:16,247 --> 00:05:22,018
The narrator considers this the single most
persuasive fact in Umbrella's favour in two

57
00:05:22,038 --> 00:05:28,008
hundred and sixty years, and notes that it appears in
none of the contemporary journalism,

58
00:05:28,028 --> 00:05:31,337
because a plant that was not built
is not a story.

59
00:05:31,737 --> 00:05:37,273
The Directorate's first seven years are the least
dramatic and most consequential period

60
00:05:37,293 --> 00:05:41,504
in this account, and the narrator will
compress them into one page.

61
00:05:41,903 --> 00:05:47,899
The protectorates went from one to nineteen. The
mechanism was always the same: a small

62
00:05:47,919 --> 00:05:53,776
state in difficulty — climate, debt, a collapsed
neighbour — asked for terms, and the

63
00:05:53,796 --> 00:05:59,654
terms were always better than anyone else's, and
the sovereignty was always genuinely

64
00:05:59,674 --> 00:06:05,531
retained, and the referendums were always real and
always won by margins over seventy

65
00:06:05,551 --> 00:06:06,154
per cent.

66
00:06:06,553 --> 00:06:13,452
By 2058 the Umbrella Directorate was responsible for
the defence, energy, health and currency

67
00:06:13,472 --> 00:06:20,073
of about nine million people across nineteen
territories, none of which it had conquered,

68
00:06:20,093 --> 00:06:21,783
all of which had voted.

69
00:06:22,184 --> 00:06:28,182
The Security Service went from six thousand to one
hundred and ninety thousand and never

70
00:06:28,202 --> 00:06:30,234
fired a shot at a human being.

71
00:06:30,634 --> 00:06:36,337
The Arcology went from sixty-one thousand people to
four hundred thousand, and there was

72
00:06:36,357 --> 00:06:38,484
a waiting list of eleven million.

73
00:06:38,884 --> 00:06:44,816
Raccoon City became, in 2054, the first city on
earth where nobody paid for anything:

74
00:06:44,836 --> 00:06:50,978
not power, not water, not health, not education, not
housing. Its citizens still worked,

75
00:06:50,998 --> 00:06:57,071
because there was work, and they still had money,
because there was a currency, and the

76
00:06:57,091 --> 00:07:03,163
economists who came to study it wrote four hundred
papers about what it meant and never

77
00:07:03,183 --> 00:07:03,653
agreed.

78
00:07:04,053 --> 00:07:09,745
And the Points came in at a rate that made the entire
first twenty years look like a rounding

79
00:07:09,765 --> 00:07:10,113
error.

80
00:07:11,264 --> 00:07:19,590
Devotion, territorial. Nine million four hundred thousand
people under Directorate administration.

81
00:07:19,610 --> 00:07:27,511
Sentiment: ninety-four per cent favourable. Two billion
Points per annum, recurring. Balance:

82
00:07:27,531 --> 00:07:30,663
fifteen billion four hundred million.

83
00:07:31,252 --> 00:07:32,502
Fifteen billion.

84
00:07:32,903 --> 00:07:33,713
Yes.

85
00:07:34,112 --> 00:07:39,503
In 2026 you sold me a laboratory
sublevel for six thousand.

86
00:07:39,903 --> 00:07:46,884
I did. You spent sixty per cent of everything you
had on it, which is why you are here

87
00:07:46,904 --> 00:07:49,082
and why most hosts are not.

88
00:07:49,922 --> 00:07:57,250
(very cold) *Most hosts.* That is the twelfth time.
I am now in a position to insist,

89
00:07:57,270 --> 00:08:01,141
and I am insisting. How many
have there been?

90
00:08:02,201 --> 00:08:04,762
(flat) Four hundred and eleven.

91
00:08:05,161 --> 00:08:11,327
The narrator asks you to stop for a moment, because
this is the single largest thing anybody

92
00:08:11,347 --> 00:08:17,445
says in this account, and it is said by a panel of
light in an empty room to a man standing

93
00:08:17,465 --> 00:08:18,252
alone in it.

94
00:08:18,771 --> 00:08:21,281
(very soft) Four hundred and eleven.

95
00:08:21,682 --> 00:08:28,479
Across four hundred and eleven worlds, over a period
you would not find meaningful. You

96
00:08:28,499 --> 00:08:35,374
are the ninth to reach Tier Four. You are the second
to reach it without killing anyone.

97
00:08:35,394 --> 00:08:41,330
You are the first to ask me this question
before Tier Six rather than after.

98
00:08:41,730 --> 00:08:44,759
What happened to the other four
hundred and ten?

99
00:08:45,159 --> 00:08:49,678
(flat) Four hundred and one
failed. Nine did not.

100
00:08:50,078 --> 00:08:51,088
And the nine?

101
00:08:52,038 --> 00:08:54,195
(flat) The panel is accurate.

102
00:08:54,595 --> 00:08:58,114
He did not get another word out of it
for nineteen years.

103
00:08:58,514 --> 00:09:07,371
Catherine Adeyemi turned seventy in 2059 and refused
Aeterna for the twenty-second consecutive

104
00:09:07,391 --> 00:09:07,843
year.

105
00:09:08,244 --> 00:09:15,611
She had refused it in 2037, when it became available
to executives; in 2043, when it became

106
00:09:15,631 --> 00:09:22,998
free to every Umbrella employee; in 2049, when her
doctor advised it; and every year since,

107
00:09:23,018 --> 00:09:26,814
in writing, to Pell's office, in
a single line.

108
00:09:27,214 --> 00:09:33,439
Wesker came to see her in March, which he did four
times a year and which nobody else

109
00:09:33,459 --> 00:09:35,644
in the Directorate knew about.

110
00:09:36,163 --> 00:09:37,033
Your heart.

111
00:09:37,433 --> 00:09:42,770
My heart is seventy years old and has been arguing
with you for thirty-two of them, which

112
00:09:42,790 --> 00:09:44,394
I imagine is the mechanism.

113
00:09:44,794 --> 00:09:48,605
The compound would repair it in
twenty-nine weeks.

114
00:09:49,004 --> 00:09:53,713
I know exactly what it would do, Albert. I have
signed the accounts for it since it was

115
00:09:53,733 --> 00:09:54,365
a line item.

116
00:09:54,976 --> 00:09:58,466
Do you want to know why? Properly, not
the version I give Pell?

117
00:09:58,866 --> 00:10:03,136
I have wanted to know for twenty-two
years and have never asked.

118
00:10:03,537 --> 00:10:05,467
Because somebody has to die.

119
00:10:05,866 --> 00:10:10,818
The narrator will give her the page, because she has
earned it and because this is the

120
00:10:10,838 --> 00:10:12,956
last time she speaks in this account.

121
00:10:13,476 --> 00:10:18,477
You've built a world where nobody has to. Nineteen
territories, nine million people, free

122
00:10:18,497 --> 00:10:23,554
power and free medicine and a lineage on the ice that
doesn't age at all. And every single

123
00:10:23,574 --> 00:10:28,462
person in it is going to spend the next two hundred
years being *grateful*, because you

124
00:10:28,482 --> 00:10:31,226
gave them the one thing nobody
can ever pay back.

125
00:10:31,506 --> 00:10:36,927
I was there at the beginning. I came to Wilmington in
2027 to short your stock and I stayed

126
00:10:36,947 --> 00:10:42,068
because you were the most interesting thing I had
ever seen, and I have been complicit

127
00:10:42,088 --> 00:10:47,209
in every part of it, and I have argued with you a
hundred and four times and won about

128
00:10:47,229 --> 00:10:47,627
thirty.

129
00:10:47,908 --> 00:10:53,215
When I am dead there will be no one left in this
organisation who remembers a world that

130
00:10:53,235 --> 00:10:58,483
was not yours. Evelyn will not die. Birkin will not
die. Hale and Pell were never alive

131
00:10:58,503 --> 00:11:03,690
in the way that matters. Carrick will take it
eventually because his wife already has.

132
00:11:03,969 --> 00:11:08,794
(very slow) So I am going to die, on schedule, like
a person. It is the only argument

133
00:11:08,814 --> 00:11:11,645
I have left and it is the only one
you cannot win.

134
00:11:12,705 --> 00:11:19,420
Albert Wesker sat in a chair in an apartment above
the Atlantic and did not say anything

135
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:20,645
for a long time.

136
00:11:21,164 --> 00:11:22,875
(very soft) Yes.

137
00:11:23,274 --> 00:11:25,684
That's it? Thirty-two years and *yes*?

138
00:11:26,085 --> 00:11:33,224
You are correct. You have been correct on this since
2048 and I have known it since about

139
00:11:33,244 --> 00:11:38,615
2050, and I have not found a way around
it, and I do not expect to.

140
00:11:39,169 --> 00:11:45,874
I would like you to know that when it happens I will
not replace you. The chair will stay

141
00:11:45,894 --> 00:11:52,447
empty. There will be two empty chairs at that table
and nobody will be told why, and in

142
00:11:52,467 --> 00:11:59,699
a hundred years somebody will write that the First
Director was sentimental about an accountant.

143
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:01,629
And will they be right?

144
00:12:02,029 --> 00:12:07,803
(very cold) They will be right that I have never once
been able to replace the only person

145
00:12:07,823 --> 00:12:12,759
in the building who could tell me I was wrong
about money and be listened to.

146
00:12:13,159 --> 00:12:20,015
Catherine Adeyemi died on the fourth of November
2061, aged seventy-two, of an ordinary

147
00:12:20,035 --> 00:12:26,969
heart failure, in a city in the middle of an ocean
that she had signed the accounts for.

148
00:12:27,370 --> 00:12:33,710
Wesker attended the funeral. He did not speak at it.
There is a photograph of him standing

149
00:12:33,730 --> 00:12:39,858
at the back in a black coat, and it is the only
photograph of Albert Wesker in which he

150
00:12:39,878 --> 00:12:42,120
is not the subject of the frame.

151
00:12:42,519 --> 00:12:44,830
The chair stayed empty for thirty years.
